\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every fixed shift $\rho \in (0,1)$, do the matrices indexed by odd vertex sets U and perfect matchings M, with entry $|M\cap \delta (U)|-1+\rho $, where $\delta (U)$ is the set of edges crossing U, of complete graphs on even n vertices require real positive-semidefinite factorization rank at least $2^{cn}$, for one $c>0$ independent of $\rho $ and all sufficiently large n depending on $\rho $? This also asks for the resulting exponential exact semidefinite-lift lower bound.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every fixed shift $\rho \in (0,1)$, do the matrices indexed by odd vertex sets U and perfect matchings M, with entry $|M\cap \delta (U)|-1+\rho $, where $\delta (U)$ is the set of edges crossing U, of complete graphs on even n vertices require real positive-semidefinite factorization rank at least $2^{cn}$, for one $c>0$ independent of $\rho $ and all sufficiently large n depending on $\rho $? This also asks for the resulting exponential exact semidefinite-lift lower bound.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.01929}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

