\section*{Exponential semidefinite complexity for perfect matching}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 126; 2026-10-05), the asserted result is that each fixed positive shift below one in the matching slack matrix has exponential real PSD rank, implying exponential size for exact semidefinite lifts of the matching polytope.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 126; 2026-10-05), coordinatewise product averages are expressed as sums of matrix squares. Orthogonal frequency splitting bounds a Gram potential through the averaging process; matching smoothing and an expanding parity system force a rank large enough to support the remaining Fourier mass.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 126; 2026-10-05).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 126; 2026-10-05), the documented Lean scope is superpolynomial PSD rank for the selected matching slack matrix and superpolynomial size of exact lifts; the claimed exponential bound for every fixed positive shift is outside the selected statement.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{MatchingAffineLift.lean}, \texttt{MatchingPSD.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.PerfectMatchingPSD.affine\_lift\_lower\_bound}, \texttt{OAI.PerfectMatchingPSD.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

